findings.body:
The else branch estimates the listpack total by averaging the first `samples` listpacks, multiplying by the number of listpacks, and then adding the real size of the last listpack. The PR changes the multiplier from `numele-1` to `numele`, so the last listpack is counted twice.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1326-1337"}

The comment above the loop (lines 1311-1314) describes exactly the N-1 average plus real-last-node approach, so the code now contradicts its comment. The overstatement is about one average listpack per call. It is small for large streams, but it can be a sizeable share of the estimate for streams with just over the sample threshold (the else branch is taken only when `numele > samples`, so 6 or more listpacks with the default sample size of 5).

The PR updates the comments in `tests/unit/type/list.tcl` and `tests/unit/type/hash.tcl` to say they cover `kvobjComputeSize`, but the assertions are still `assert_morethan [memory_usage k] 0`. That passes for any non-zero value, including the quicklist overestimate and the stream double-count described above. Nothing in the suite checks the quantitative accuracy of the quicklist or stream branches.

::diff{file="tests/unit/type/list.tcl" lines="2243-2247"}

The new string test shows the right pattern: bounds on the result. The same approach would catch both regressions.
